      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-4z &=-10 \\\\ y+z &=4 \\\\ z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -4 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nR_{2}:=R_{2}-R_{3},\\qquad M\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -4 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n$$\nR_{1}:=R_{1}+4R_{3},\\qquad M\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=6 \\\\ y &=0 \\\\ z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=6$, $y=0$, and $z=4$. Or, in vector form, $\\langle 6,0,4\\rangle$.",
